  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\nThe optimal solution for the minimum overlap problem is achieved by constructing a function that balances the occurrence of both h(x) = 1 and h(x+k) = 0 across all shifts. We use a geometry-based construction: divide [0,2] into 6 intervals and assign a binary-like pattern (0.25 or 0.75) with periodicity to create a function that minimizes the maximum overlap. This differs from prior optimization-based approaches by leveraging symmetry and constructibility to escape local optima.\n\nKey improvements over the parent solution:\n- Fixed structure ensuring global feasibility\n- Automated resolution selection based on symmetry\n- Tailored smooth-max formulation\n- Restorable design from parent solution\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n- Representation: Discrete samples from a periodic function with period 1/3\n- Objective: minimize C5 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx over [0,2]\n- Constraints: \n  - Integral preservation: \u222bh(x)dx = 1\n  - Bounds: 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1\n- Smooth surrogate:  \n  C5_smooth = inf_{\u03c4>0} [ (max_correlation + \u03c4 log \u03a3e^{(corr_{k}-max_corr)/\u03c4)} ] * dx\n  \nMeasure preserving via iterative scaling:  \nInitialize with period structure, repeatedly scale+clip to 1 integral over [0,2].\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n```python\ndef construct_base_pattern():\n    T = 3  # Periodicity period (arbitrary choice)\n    base = [0.25, 0.75, 0.25, 0.75, 0.25, 0.75]  # Fundamental pattern\n    return pm.periodic_expand(base, T)  # Expand by T periods\n```\n\n4. Search Choices:\n- Resolutions: 270, 540, 1080 (multiples of 3)\n- Base pattern: Fixed [0.25, 0.75] with period -6\n- \u03c4 schedule: [0.5, 0.1, 0.05] for smoothing\n- Perturbations: Add cyclical noise with \u03ba=0.02 to maintain period\n\n5. Compute Budget:\n- Score calculation: O(n^2)\n- Optimization: SLSQP + successive approximation\n- Budget division: 300s for coarse, 600s for medium, 400s for fine\n\n6. Validation:\n  - Fallback: Store initial Her solution (n=180)\n  - Iterative repair:\n    def check feasibility(h):\n        return all(0.2 <= h_i <= 0.8 for h_i in h)",
